<p style="font-size:0.85em;color:#666;margin:0 0 16px;">Updated March 2026. Written by the Real Skin Care editorial team with input from our product formulation staff. We sell SLS-free toothpaste — we've noted that clearly so you can weigh our perspective accordingly.</p>

<section style="border:1px solid #eee;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;margin:0 0 20px;background:#fafafa;text-align:center;">
  <h2 style="margin:0 0 8px;">Clean Teeth Without the Harsh Foam</h2>
  <p style="margin:0 0 12px;">Our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/products/coconut-oil-toothpaste">coconut oil toothpaste</a> is completely SLS-free — made with simple, natural ingredients your mouth will love.</p>
  <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/products/coconut-oil-toothpaste" style="display:inline-block;padding:12px 18px;border-radius:8px;background-color:#AEDEAC;color:#000;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;">Shop SLS-Free Toothpaste</a>
</section>

<p>Yes — SLS-free toothpaste cleans just as well as regular toothpaste. The foam that SLS creates feels like it's doing the work, but cleaning actually comes from abrasives and active ingredients. Removing SLS doesn't reduce how well a toothpaste cleans your teeth. What it does remove is a known irritant that a growing number of dentists now flag as unnecessary, especially for people with sensitive mouths or dry mouth.</p>

<p>This post covers what SLS is, why it's in most toothpastes, what the real documented concerns are, and who should consider switching to an SLS-free formula.</p>

<h2>What Is SLS in Toothpaste?</h2>

<p>Sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS) is a surfactant — an ingredient that helps liquids spread and lift away oils and debris. In toothpaste, it creates foam and helps other ingredients distribute evenly across your teeth. You'll find it in shampoos and body washes for the same reason: it's a cheap, effective foaming agent.</p>

<p>Chemically, SLS is derived from lauryl alcohol, which can come from coconut oil, palm oil, or petroleum depending on the manufacturer.</p>

<p><strong>How to spot SLS on a label:</strong> Look for <em>sodium lauryl sulfate</em>, <em>sodium dodecyl sulfate</em>, or the abbreviations <em>SLS</em> or <em>SDS</em>.</p>

<p>Note: SLS is different from SLES (sodium laureth sulfate), which is a related but milder compound. Some brands swap one for the other — that's not the same as going sulfate-free. Always check the full ingredient list rather than relying on front-of-pack claims.</p>

<h2>Why Do Brands Use SLS?</h2>

<p>There are practical reasons SLS became a staple in toothpaste. Consumers associate thick lather with cleaning power — foam feels like something is happening. SLS also helps active ingredients spread more evenly across tooth surfaces, which is a real functional benefit. And it's inexpensive and shelf-stable at scale.</p>

<p>But here's the key point: foam has nothing to do with cleaning. That's handled by abrasives and active ingredients. The foam is a sensory cue — one we've been conditioned to expect. SLS-free toothpastes foam less or not at all, and that can feel strange at first. It doesn't mean they're less effective.</p>

<h2>Does SLS-Free Toothpaste Clean as Well as Regular Toothpaste?</h2>

<p>Yes. The cleaning action in toothpaste comes from mild abrasives (like baking soda or hydrated silica) and active ingredients — not from foam. SLS is a foaming agent, not a cleaning agent. Removing it doesn't reduce the toothpaste's ability to remove plaque or debris.</p>

<p>In fact, some SLS-free formulas clean more gently because they don't disrupt the mouth's natural mucosal barrier the way SLS does. For most people, switching to SLS-free toothpaste means the same cleaning result with less irritation potential. See our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/best-toothpaste-without-sls-2025">best toothpaste without SLS guide</a> for specific product comparisons.</p>

<h2>Is SLS in Toothpaste Bad for You?</h2>

<p>SLS is not acutely dangerous for most people. It's approved for use in oral care products at concentrations around 1–2%. You're not swallowing it. Regulatory bodies in the US and EU have found no credible evidence of cancer or systemic harm at these levels.</p>

<p>That said, "not dangerous" isn't the same as "harmless." The documented concerns are real — they're just more mundane than scary headlines suggest.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Mouth irritation:</strong> SLS can disrupt the mucosal lining inside your cheeks, lips, and gums — the protective barrier that keeps soft tissue healthy. Daily exposure gradually weakens that barrier.</li>
  <li><strong>Dry mouth:</strong> SLS breaks down the film that helps your mouth stay lubricated. If you already have dry mouth (xerostomia), daily SLS exposure makes it worse. Dry mouth raises your cavity risk because saliva is a key defense against bacteria. See more on <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/7-ingredients-to-avoid-in-natural-toothpaste">ingredients to avoid in natural toothpaste</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Skin reactions around the mouth:</strong> SLS residue near the lips can trigger perioral dermatitis — a rash-like irritation around the mouth and chin — or worsen existing eczema.</li>
</ul>

<p>Many dentists now recommend SLS-free formulas as a default for patients with recurrent oral irritation, canker sores, or chronic dry mouth.</p>

<h2>Can SLS Cause Canker Sores?</h2>

<p>The evidence here is more specific than for other SLS concerns. A 1994 study published in the <em>Journal of Clinical Periodontology</em> (Herlofson &amp; Barkvoll) found that patients using SLS-containing toothpaste got significantly more canker sores than those using an SLS-free formula. The proposed reason: SLS strips the protective mucin coating from the mouth's lining, leaving tissue more exposed and vulnerable.</p>

<p>Later research has broadly supported this, though canker sores are multifactorial — SLS is unlikely to be the only cause for everyone.</p>

<p>If you get canker sores regularly, switching to SLS-free toothpaste is one of the simplest things you can try. Give it four to six weeks. Some people notice a real reduction in frequency. Others don't — which may mean their canker sores have a different primary trigger. Either way, there's no downside to trying, since SLS serves no therapeutic purpose in toothpaste.</p>

