<h2>Is Sulfate-Free Toothpaste Better for Dry Mouth?</h2>

<p>Yes — for people with dry mouth, switching to a sulfate-free toothpaste is one of the most practical changes you can make. SLS degrades the mucosal film that helps keep your mouth lubricated and retain saliva. If dry mouth is already an issue, using an SLS-containing toothpaste twice a day makes it harder for your body to compensate.</p>

<p>Dry mouth (xerostomia) isn't just uncomfortable — reduced saliva flow increases your risk of cavities and gum problems because saliva neutralizes acids and washes away bacteria. Removing SLS from your toothpaste eliminates one daily irritant that actively works against that process. For more on this, see our guide to <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/best-toothpaste-for-sensitive-teeth-2025">best toothpaste for sensitive teeth</a>.</p>

<h2>Is Sensodyne SLS-Free — or Does It Depend on the Variant?</h2>

<p>It depends on the variant. Some Sensodyne formulas — including Sensodyne Pronamel — are SLS-free, which is part of why they're recommended for sensitive teeth. Other Sensodyne products do contain SLS. The brand has moved away from SLS in several of its lines in response to sensitivity concerns, but not across the board.</p>

<p>The safest approach: check the ingredient list of the specific Sensodyne product you're looking at, rather than assuming the whole range is SLS-free. Look for <em>sodium lauryl sulfate</em> or <em>sodium dodecyl sulfate</em> in the inactive ingredients.</p>

<h2>Who Should Switch to SLS-Free Toothpaste?</h2>

<p>Some people have clear reasons to make the switch. Others simply want to reduce unnecessary irritants in their daily routine. Here's who benefits most:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Frequent canker sores:</strong> SLS-free toothpaste is one of the first things worth trying if you get them regularly.</li>
  <li><strong>Sensitive teeth or gums:</strong> SLS can make gum irritation worse. A gentle, SLS-free formula removes that variable.</li>
  <li><strong>Dry mouth:</strong> SLS degrades the mucosal film that helps retain saliva. Removing it is a simple, low-effort swap.</li>
  <li><strong>Perioral dermatitis or lip irritation:</strong> SLS residue around the lip line is a known trigger. If you've had unexplained chin or jawline skin issues, your toothpaste could be contributing.</li>
  <li><strong>Known SLS sensitivity:</strong> Symptoms can include redness, swelling, peeling, or a burning sensation in or around the mouth. If that sounds familiar, switching is straightforward.</li>
  <li><strong>Children:</strong> Kids have thinner mucosal tissue and often swallow some toothpaste. Many pediatric dentists now recommend SLS-free formulas as a precaution. See our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/best-natural-toothpaste-for-families-2025">best natural toothpaste for families</a> guide.</li>
  <li><strong>Anyone moving toward a cleaner routine:</strong> There's no downside to removing SLS. It cleans just as well without it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to Look for in an SLS-Free Toothpaste</h2>

<p>Going SLS-free is the right starting point. Here's what else to check:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>What replaces SLS:</strong> Some brands use gentler surfactants like sodium cocoyl glutamate or decyl glucoside. Others — like our own formula — use organic coconut oil as the base, which contributes to texture and spread without a harsh surfactant. More on that in our post on <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/can-coconut-oil-replace-toothpaste-discover-the-benefits-of-this-natural-alternative">using coconut oil as toothpaste</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Fluoride vs. fluoride-free:</strong> Going SLS-free doesn't mean giving up fluoride — these are separate decisions. Plenty of SLS-free toothpastes include fluoride; plenty don't. See our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/fluoride-free-toothpaste-benefits-how-it-works-best-picks">fluoride-free toothpaste guide</a> if you're exploring that route.</li>
  <li><strong>Other ingredients worth skipping:</strong> While you're reading the label, look out for artificial sweeteners like saccharin, artificial dyes, and carrageenan. A clean formula should have a short, readable ingredient list.</li>
  <li><strong>Functional natural actives:</strong> Baking soda is mildly abrasive, helps neutralize acids, and has a well-documented role in oral care. Ingredients like clove, cinnamon, and myrrh have histories of use in traditional oral care and are studied for antimicrobial properties. When you see them in a formula, they're earning their place. See also: <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/why-glycerin-free-toothpaste-matters">why glycerin-free toothpaste matters</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>One practical tip:</strong> Don't rely on front-of-pack claims. "Natural" and "gentle" mean nothing legally. Flip the tube and check the ingredient list for <em>sodium lauryl sulfate</em>, <em>sodium dodecyl sulfate</em>, or <em>SDS</em>. If any appear, the toothpaste is not SLS-free regardless of what the marketing says. For a curated list of verified options, see our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/best-natural-toothpaste-2025">best natural toothpaste</a> roundup.</p>

