<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Does SLS-free toothpaste clean as well as regular toothpaste?</h3>
<p>Yes. Cleaning comes from abrasives and active ingredients, not foam. SLS creates the foam but doesn't contribute to plaque removal. An SLS-free toothpaste with the right abrasives cleans just as effectively.</p>

<h3>Is SLS-free toothpaste better for dry mouth?</h3>
<p>Yes. SLS disrupts the mucosal film that helps your mouth stay lubricated. For anyone with dry mouth (xerostomia), switching to a sulfate-free toothpaste removes one daily trigger that worsens the condition.</p>

<h3>Is Sensodyne SLS-free?</h3>
<p>Some variants are, some aren't. Sensodyne Pronamel is SLS-free. Other Sensodyne products contain SLS. Always check the specific product's ingredient list.</p>

<h3>What are the side effects of SLS in toothpaste?</h3>
<p>The most common are mouth irritation, worsened dry mouth, and increased canker sore frequency. Some people also experience skin reactions around the mouth. These effects vary by individual — many people use SLS toothpaste without noticeable issues.</p>

<h3>Can I get a natural SLS-free toothpaste that still works?</h3>
<p>Yes. Look for formulas with baking soda as the primary abrasive and coconut oil or a gentle plant-based surfactant as the base. Avoid formulas that swap SLS for SLES — that's still a sulfate. Our <a href="https://www.realskincare.com/blogs/news/7-ingredients-to-avoid-in-natural-toothpaste">guide to ingredients to avoid in natural toothpaste</a> covers what to watch for.</p>

